# Part 1: title, header, abstract, §1.0 (new), §1.1–§1.10.
import pathlib
SRC = (pathlib.Path(__file__).parent / "v11_source.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8")

def block(start, end):
    """The text from `start` (included) to `end` (excluded); both unique in v11."""
    assert SRC.count(start) == 1, start
    assert SRC.count(end) == 1, end
    i = SRC.index(start); j = SRC.index(end, i)
    return SRC[i:j]

DATE = "4 October 2026"

OLD_ABSTRACT = block("## Abstract\n", "---\n\n## 1. Introduction and main results")

NEW_ABSTRACT = """## Abstract

Let `X ⊂ P^{n+1}` be the complex Fermat variety of degree `m ≥ 3` and even dimension `n = 2k`, and let `L(X) ⊆ H_n(X; Z)` be the subgroup generated by the classes of its standard `k`-dimensional linear subspaces. Degtyarev and Shimada conjectured that `H_n(X; Z)/L(X)` is torsion free [DS, Conjecture 1.2]. **We prove this conjecture for every degree `m ≥ 3` in every even dimension**, modulo two topological results used by [DS]: Pham's theorem on the homology of the affine Fermat variety, and their intersection numbers. Its algebraic form — the quotient `Z[G]/(ψ_J)` of the group ring of [DS] is free abelian of rank `m^{2k+1} − Q_k(m)` — is proved with no topology, and it has been verified for every degree by a complete proof in the proof assistant Lean 4 with the Mathlib library, with no gap and no axiom beyond the three standard ones. The proof reduces the conjecture, prime by prime, to dimension counts in truncated polynomial rings, proved by peeling off one variable at a time. Consequences: the integral Hodge conjecture for `X` whenever `m` is prime, or `m = 4`, or `m` is prime to `(n+1)!`, with the integral Hodge classes of middle degree generated by the standard linear subspaces — in particular for the Fermat quartic of every even dimension; and the primitivity of the linear subspaces of the partial Fermat varieties of [DS], which [DS] proved conditionally on the conjecture. This work has not been refereed by a human expert.

"""

SUMMARY = """### 1.0 Summary of results

`m ≥ 3` is the degree, `n = 2k` the dimension, `N = 2k + 2`, and `Q_k(m) = |Γ_𝒥|` is the number of §1.2. «Lean» means that the statement is proved in Lean 4 with Mathlib, with no gap and no axiom beyond `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` (§14.8, [LC]).

| Result | What it says | Range | What it rests on | Lean |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Main Theorem′** (§1.2) | `R/(ψ_J : J ∈ 𝒥)` is free abelian of rank `m^{2k+1} − Q_k(m)`; the same dimension over every field | every `m ≥ 3`, `k ≥ 1` | nothing outside this paper: the upper bound is [DS, Claim 4.3], a count of points re-proved in Appendix B, Step 6 | yes, every `m ≥ 1`, `k ≥ 0` |
| **Main Theorem** = Conjecture 1.2 of [DS] (§1.2) | `H_{2k}(X; Z)/L(X)` is torsion free, and `rank L(X) = Q_k(m) + 1` | every `m ≥ 3`, `k ≥ 0` | Main Theorem′ and [DS, Theorems 1.1(a), 1.4], which Appendix B derives from Pham's theorem [Ph] and [DS, Theorem 2.2] | the algebraic form only |
| **Corollary H** (§1.3, §12) | `L(X)` is primitive in `Hdg(X)` with free quotient of rank `|𝔅| − Q_k(m)`; `Hdg(X) = L(X)`, and the integral Hodge conjecture holds, iff `m` is prime, `m = 4`, or `gcd(m, (n+1)!) = 1` | every `m ≥ 3`, `k ≥ 1` | the Main Theorem; the classical facts (H1)–(H3); Aoki's theorem (H5) [Aok] (for prime `m` and `m = 4`, the proofs (H4), (H4′) given here) | no |
| **Corollary W** (§1.4, §13) | the linear subspaces of [DS] on the partial Fermat varieties span primitive sublattices | every `m ≥ 3`, `0 ≤ s ≤ d` | the Main Theorem; part (i) also the transport argument of [DS, §4.6] | no |
| **Theorem B** (§1.5, §5) | the count at the even box `q − 1`, with equality and a free quotient over `Z` | every odd `q ≥ 3`, every field, every `k` | — | yes |
| **Theorem C** (§7) | the bipartite counts, `≥`; equality over `F̄_p` (Corollaries 7.8, 9.12) | every `q ≥ 2`, fields with `C(q − 1, t) ≠ 0` | — | yes |
| **Theorem O** (§1.5, §8) | the count at the odd box `r`, with equality and a free quotient over `Z` | every odd `r ≥ 3`, every field, every `k` | — | yes |
| **Theorem A** (§1.6, §10) | a different count; it does not imply the conjecture; as a count it is due to Kostant (characteristic `0`) and to [BRR] (odd characteristic) | `char F ≠ 2`, odd `q` | — (Appendix A) | no |
| **Corollary 10.8** | at the odd box, sum and intersection of the matching ideals coincide | `char F ≠ 2`, odd `r` | Theorems O and A | no |
| **Fact 11.2** | the analogue of the Main Theorem for subfamilies of matchings is false | a computation | — | no |

Everything in this table that is not marked «Lean» was read cold by readers other than its writer (§14.6). No human expert has refereed this work. What we found in the literature, and where we searched, is in §1.10.

"""

