LIT = """
### 1.10 Literature and priority
Before this version we searched the literature for each result that we call new: the arXiv listings and its index of titles and abstracts; Google Scholar and the open web; the papers that cite [DS], through Semantic Scholar and OpenAlex; the publication lists of the authors of [DS]; MathOverflow; and GitHub, including Mathlib and DeepMind's `formal-conjectures` repository, for formalizations. The queries, their dates and their results are recorded in [Rep], `record/literature/`. The sources that decide an attribution were read in the original; the others are cited for the statement they make, and the list of references says which is which. What we found:
- *The even degrees.* To the best of our knowledge, no proof of Conjecture 1.2 for an even degree `m ≥ 4` and `k ≥ 1` had appeared before this paper, beyond the cells confirmed by computer in [DS, §5] (and the case `k = 1`, which is [De14]). The conjecture is listed as open in [De15, Conjecture 4.4].
- *The odd box.* Over `Q`, Theorem O and Corollary 10.8 can be derived from Kostant's theorem and the first fundamental theorem of invariant theory for the orthogonal group (Remark 10.7(2)); we did not find this derivation written. In positive characteristic we did not find Theorem O, or the statement that the tensors `D_{r,J}` generate the invariants of the centralizer of a regular unipotent element (Corollary 10.8 and Remark 10.7), in the literature, in particular not in [BRR], [Ric] or [Liu]. To the best of our knowledge, both are new in positive characteristic.
- *Corollary H.* The implication from the primitivity of `L(X)` to `Hdg(X) = L(X)` in the degrees of (i) is the argument of [AMV, §1], with the degrees of Aoki's theorem [Aok]; for `k = 1` the statement is known ([Miz], [SSvL], [De14]); for `m = 4` and `k = 2, 3`, and in the prime cells of [DS, §5], it already followed from computations (§1.3). For `k ≥ 2` the uniform statement — every prime `m`, the quartics of dimension at least `8`, every non-prime `m` of (i), and part (ii) — is, to the best of our knowledge, new.
- *Corollary W.* The implication «Conjecture 1.2 in dimension `2s` ⟹ (i)» is [DS, Corollary 1.7]; what is new is that the hypothesis is now a theorem.
- *Theorem A* is due, as a count, to Kostant and to [BRR] (Remark 10.4); our contribution there is the dictionary and an elementary proof.
- *Pfaffians.* The properties (i)–(iii), (F1)–(F4) and (F7) of §8.3 are classical; we cite [Knu] and [Oka]. We did not find the rank-two update of Lemma 8.8 in the sources we read, and we claim nothing for it: it is of the kind of the classical expansions of the Pfaffian of a sum of two alternating matrices, which we have not traced to a source that we have read. Its use to prove the membership lemma (Lemmas 8.7, 8.7′) is ours.
- *Orbit harmonics.* The lower bounds by degenerating the ideal of a finite set of points to the ideal of its top-degree forms (Lemma 5.8′, Proposition A.5) are the classical method of orbit harmonics (see [GP]).
- *Formalization.* Mathlib has no Pfaffian; we defined Pfaffians and bordered Pfaffians in our project. A separate partial formalization of skew-symmetric matrices and Pfaffians exists outside Mathlib (`Qsdd/Skewsymmetric` on GitHub). We found no formalization of results of the kind of [DS]; to the best of our knowledge, §14.8 is the first machine-checked proof of a case of Conjecture 1.2.
- *Related work on the rational Hodge conjecture:* [Jum], [MMRV], [FK] (§1.3).

"""
